# Season 1 additions, batch 2 (29 Oct 2026). 'after_slug' = slug of the write-up it follows (source numbering); gen2.py resolves and renumbers.
A = [
dict(after_slug="ai-agent", slug="agent-harness", title="What Is an Agent Harness, and Why Does Every Agent Need One?", topic="Agent harness", key="agent harness",
buzz='"Agent harness." The software around the model that lets an agent act safely. Every serious agent has one.',
hw='A racing car engine is powerful, but nobody can drive it without a steering wheel, brakes and a dashboard.\n\nThat\'s the hello world: an **agent harness** is the software around the model that gives it tools, limits, memory and a way to stop.',
step='The model only predicts text. The harness runs the loop: it sends the model its instructions, carries out the tool calls it asks for (write-up 63), checks what came back, keeps the record, and decides when the task is done.\n\nA good harness also sets limits: how many steps an agent may take, which files it can touch, and when a person must approve. That\'s why two agents using the same model can behave very differently.',
met='Coding agents that run tests and edit files, and assistants that show you each step before they act.',
name='An **agent harness**, sometimes called an **agent runtime**.',
words=[("Harness","the software around a model that runs and limits it."),("Tool loop","the cycle of asking the model, running a tool and reading the result."),("Stop condition","the rule that tells an agent when the task is finished.")],
notit='The harness isn\'t the model, and a better model doesn\'t replace a missing harness. Without limits, a capable model can still take a wrong step many times in a row.',
take=["Ask what limits your agent has, and who can stop it.","Count the steps an agent takes before a person checks.","Test the harness, not only the model."],
close="The engine is the model. The harness is the steering wheel and brakes.",
q="Would you trust an agent that has no limits on what it can do?",
scene='A racing car engine, with no steering wheel or brakes.\nPowerful, and nobody can drive it.',
hook="An agent harness is the steering wheel and brakes around the model.",
extra="Limits make agents useful, not just powerful."),

dict(after_slug="llmops", slug="observability", title="What Is Observability, and Why Can't You Debug an AI Without It?", topic="Observability", key="observability",
buzz='"Observability." Seeing inside a running system. For AI, it\'s how you find out why an answer went wrong.',
hw='A car with no dashboard. It might run fine for months, but when it breaks, you have no idea why, because you never saw the speed, the temperature or the warning lights.\n\nThat\'s the hello world: **observability** is being able to see what a system did and why, from the records it leaves behind.',
step='For AI, observability records each step of a request: the prompt, the documents retrieved, each tool call, the model used, the answer, the time and the cost. These records are called traces. Dashboards and alerts turn them into signals, such as a jump in wrong answers or in cost per question.\n\nWithout it, a bad answer is a mystery. With it, you can see which step went wrong and fix that step, not guess.',
met='The "view trace" panels in AI tools, and the dashboards that show cost and speed per request.',
name='**Observability**, which relies on **traces**, **logs** and **metrics**.',
words=[("Trace","the recorded steps behind one request."),("Log","a timestamped record of an event."),("Metric","a number tracked over time, such as cost or speed.")],
notit='Observability isn\'t the same as monitoring a single number. A green dashboard can hide a wrong answer. You need to see the steps, not only the total.',
take=["Log each step of an AI request, not only the final answer.","Keep a trace for every bad answer a user reports.","Alert on trends, such as rising errors or cost."],
close="No record, no explanation.",
q="When an AI gives a wrong answer at your work, can anyone see why?",
scene='The AI gave a wrong answer last Tuesday.\nNobody knows why. There is no record.',
hook="Observability is seeing inside an AI system, so you can debug it.",
extra="No records, no explanations."),

dict(after_slug="ai-memory", slug="checkpoint", title="What Is a Checkpoint, and Why Is It Better Than Starting Over?", topic="Checkpoint", key="checkpoint",
buzz='"Checkpoint." A saved point an AI agent can resume from. Long tasks depend on them.',
hw='A video game with save points. Lose a level and you return to the last save, not to the title screen, and you keep your progress.\n\nThat\'s the hello world: a **checkpoint** is a saved snapshot of an agent\'s progress, so it can resume after an error or a pause instead of starting over.',
step='A long agent task might take an hour and many tool calls. The harness saves the state at key steps: what has been done, what the results were, and what comes next. If the network fails or a person pauses the task, the agent loads the last checkpoint and continues.\n\nCheckpoints also allow a human to inspect the state, correct it and resume. Saving everything costs storage, so teams choose the steps that matter.',
met='An agent that picks up a long report where it left off after a crash, and a "continue from here" button in AI tools.',
name='A **checkpoint**, also called a **save point**.',
words=[("Checkpoint","a saved snapshot of an agent's progress."),("Resume","continuing a task from a saved point."),("State","everything an agent needs to know to continue.")],
notit='A checkpoint isn\'t a backup of the whole system. It saves the agent\'s progress, so a restart doesn\'t repeat finished steps or the actions it already took.',
take=["Save progress at each step that has side effects, such as sending or paying.","Check that a resumed task doesn\'t repeat finished actions.","Let a person review the saved state before resuming."],
close="Resume from the last save, not the title screen.",
q="Would you trust a long AI task that can resume after a crash?",
scene='A video game with save points.\nLose a level, and you go back to the last save.',
hook="A checkpoint lets an agent resume from its last saved step.",
extra="Resume, don't restart."),

dict(after_slug="multi-agent-system", slug="supervisor-agent", title="What Is a Supervisor Agent, and Who Decides Which Agent Does What?", topic="Supervisor agent", key="supervisor agent",
buzz='"Supervisor agent." The manager agent in a team of agents. It decides who does what, and checks the work.',
hw='A film set has a director. The director doesn\'t hold the camera or do the lighting. They decide who does what, when, and whether the take is good.\n\nThat\'s the hello world: a **supervisor agent** is the one agent that splits a task, routes each part to a specialist agent, and checks the results.',
step='The supervisor reads the request, picks which specialist should handle each part (a search agent, a coding agent, a booking agent), passes them the right context, and collects what they return. It can ask for a retry or stop the team when the work goes off track.\n\nThe risk is a supervisor that becomes a bottleneck or misroutes work. Clear roles, limits on how many agents it can start, and logs of every handoff keep the team under control.',
met='Research assistants that split a question across several agents, and travel bots that hand flights and hotels to different agents.',
name='A **supervisor agent**, also called an **orchestrator** or a **manager agent**.',
words=[("Supervisor","the agent that routes and checks the work."),("Specialist agent","an agent built for one kind of job."),("Handoff","passing a task and its context to another agent.")],
notit='A supervisor agent isn\'t automatically smarter. More agents mean more handoffs, more cost and more places for things to go wrong. Use one only when a single agent struggles.',
take=["Give each specialist one clear job.","Limit how many agents a supervisor can start.","Log every handoff so you can see who did what."],
close="One director, many specialists.",
q="Would you trust a manager agent to split your work between others?",
scene='A film director doesn\'t hold the camera.\nThey decide who does what, and check the take.',
hook="A supervisor agent is the manager that routes work to specialist agents.",
extra="Route, check, and keep a record."),

dict(after_slug="chain-of-thought", slug="reflection", title="What Is Reflection, and Why Does an AI Check Its Own Answer?", topic="Reflection", key="reflection",
buzz='"Reflection." An AI reviewing its own draft before it answers. It catches some mistakes, and misses others.',
hw='A student writes an answer, then rereads it before handing it in and spots a wrong sum.\n\nThat\'s the hello world: **reflection** is an AI looking back at its own draft, checking it against the question, and fixing what it finds.',
step='The model writes a draft, then runs a second pass that asks: is this correct, complete and in the right format? If not, it revises. Some systems use a second model as the critic, which can catch errors the first model can\'t see in its own work.\n\nReflection costs extra time and tokens. It helps most on tasks with clear checks, such as maths, code that must compile, or answers that must cite a source.',
met='Coding assistants that run their own tests and fix the errors, and AI tools that say "let me check that" before answering.',
name='**Reflection**, also called **self-critique** or **self-correction**.',
words=[("Draft","the first version an AI produces."),("Critique","a review that points out what is wrong."),("Self-correction","fixing your own output after checking it.")],
notit='Reflection isn\'t a guarantee. An AI can review its own mistake and agree with it. A check against something outside the model, such as a test or a source, is more reliable.',
take=["Use reflection where there is a clear check, such as tests or sources.","Don't rely on an AI\'s own review alone for high-stakes answers.","Compare answers with and without reflection on a sample."],
close="A draft, a second look, then the answer.",
q="Has an AI ever fixed its own mistake after you asked it to check?",
scene='A student writes an answer.\nThen rereads it and spots a wrong sum.',
hook="Reflection is an AI checking its own draft before it answers.",
extra="A second look catches some mistakes."),

dict(after_slug="context-window", slug="context-rot", title="What Is Context Rot, and Why Do Long Chats Get Worse?", topic="Context rot", key="context rot",
buzz='"Context rot." AI answers can get worse as the conversation or documents grow. Longer is not always better.',
hw='A meeting that runs for three hours. By the end, nobody remembers the first decision, and the notes have so much in them that the key point is buried.\n\nThat\'s the hello world: **context rot** is an AI\'s answers getting worse as its context fills with more text, so the important details get lost.',
step='The context window (write-up 15) has a fixed size, and the model pays attention across all of it. As the context grows, old details may be overlooked, and irrelevant text competes for attention. Research on long contexts has found that models often miss facts placed in the middle of long inputs.\n\nThe fixes are practical: keep only what matters, summarise old turns, start a fresh chat for a new task, and retrieve the right passages instead of pasting everything.',
met='Long chats where an AI forgets an earlier instruction, and tools that tell you to start a new chat when the thread gets long.',
name='**Context rot**, sometimes called **lost in the middle**.',
words=[("Context rot","answers degrading as context grows."),("Lost in the middle","facts in the middle of long input are missed more often."),("Summarising","shortening old turns into their key points.")],
notit='A bigger context window doesn\'t fix context rot by itself. A longer window can hold more text, but it doesn\'t guarantee the model uses all of it well.',
take=["Start a fresh chat when a task changes.","Keep the key instructions near the start and end of the prompt.","Summarise long threads instead of pasting everything."],
close="More text doesn't mean more understanding.",
q="Have you seen an AI forget something you said earlier in a long chat?",
scene='A three-hour meeting.\nBy the end, nobody remembers the first decision.',
hook="Context rot is an AI losing track as its context fills up.",
extra="More text is not always better."),

dict(after_slug="semantic-search", slug="hybrid-search", title="What Is Hybrid Search, and Why Use Two Ways to Search?", topic="Hybrid search", key="hybrid search",
buzz='"Hybrid search." Keyword search and meaning search, combined. It finds the exact term and the similar idea.',
hw='A library with two catalogues. One lists books by exact title and author. The other groups books by what they are about. Use both, and you find the book you named and the ones you didn\'t know to ask for.\n\nThat\'s the hello world: **hybrid search** combines keyword matching with meaning matching, so a search finds both exact words and similar ideas.',
step='Keyword search (sometimes called sparse search) is great for exact names, codes and product numbers. Semantic search (dense search) finds similar meanings even when the words differ. Hybrid search runs both and merges the two lists, using a method such as reciprocal rank fusion.\n\nIn RAG systems (write-up 53), hybrid search often beats either method alone, because each one makes up for the other\'s blind spots. A reranker can then sort the merged list.',
met='Shopping and document search that finds an exact product code and similar items, and company search tools that find both a policy number and a related policy.',
name='**Hybrid search**, combining **keyword search** and **semantic search**.',
words=[("Keyword search","finds exact words and codes."),("Dense search","matches by meaning, using embeddings."),("Rank fusion","merging two ranked lists into one.")],
notit='Hybrid search isn\'t always needed. For a small set of documents, one method may be enough. Add the second method when you see misses on exact terms or on meaning.',
take=["Test your search on exact codes and on plain questions.","Add keyword search if codes or names are missed.","Merge the results, then rerank the top few."],
close="Exact words and similar meanings, in one search.",
q="Has a search tool ever missed a code you typed in exactly?",
scene='One catalogue lists books by exact title.\nThe other groups them by what they are about.',
hook="Hybrid search combines both, so you find exact matches and similar meanings.",
extra="Keywords find the exact word. Meaning finds the rest."),

dict(after_slug="agentic-ai", slug="loop-engineering", title="What Is Loop Engineering, and Why Is It the Next Step After Prompts?", topic="Loop engineering", key="loop engineering",
buzz='"Loop engineering." After prompts and context, the newest craft: designing the cycle an agent repeats until the job is done.',
hw='A kitchen line. The chef writes one recipe, but the real skill is how orders flow, what is checked at each station, and when a dish goes back.\n\nThat\'s the hello world: **loop engineering** is designing the repeated cycle an agent runs: what it sees, what it does, how it checks itself and when it stops.',
step='Prompt engineering shapes one request. Context engineering (write-up 23) shapes what the model sees. Loop engineering shapes the whole cycle: the steps, the tools, the checks, the limits, the handoffs and the stop rules.\n\nA well-designed loop makes an agent steady. A badly designed one repeats mistakes, runs forever or spends money without progress. The loop is now often the product.',
met='Coding agents that write, test and fix code in a cycle, and research agents that search, read and refine until a stop rule is met.',
name='**Loop engineering**, the design of an **agent loop**.',
words=[("Agent loop","the repeated cycle of act, check and continue."),("Stop rule","the condition that ends the loop."),("Check step","a test or review inside the loop.")],
notit='Loop engineering isn\'t adding more steps. Every step costs time and money. A good loop has the fewest steps that still reliably finish the job.',
take=["Write down the steps, checks and stop rule for each agent.","Cap the number of loop cycles and the spend per task.","Test the loop on hard cases, not only easy ones."],
close="The recipe is the prompt. The line is the loop.",
q="Could you describe the loop your team's AI tool runs, step by step?",
scene='A kitchen line.\nThe real skill is how orders flow and what gets checked.',
hook="Loop engineering designs the cycle an agent repeats until the job is done.",
extra="Steps, checks, limits, stop rule."),
]
